{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Upon his staff</b> — upon his strength and vigor; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 21:19). And Rabbi Abraham Ibn Ezra (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 21:19) said that its reason is that he not lean upon another as is the way of the sick, but only upon himself, for then the striker is acquitted when they bring him out from prison. In my opinion, <b>his staff</b> is in its plain sense, as in \"and each man his staff in his hand by reason of old age\" (Zechariah 8:4), \"the staff of a crushed reed\" (II Kings 18:21). And Scripture says that when the injured party grows stronger and walks about continually outside in the markets and in the streets upon his staff, as is the manner of the weak who have recovered from illness, <b>then the striker shall be acquitted</b>; and it teaches that even if he was negligent with himself and died afterward from his weakness, he [the striker] is not put to death. And Scripture speaks of what ordinarily happens, for injured parties who have taken to bed do not walk about outside until their wound heals and they are out of danger. And this is the meaning of <b>and he walks about outside</b>: for if he rises and walks about in his house upon his staff, he is not acquitted.\n\nAnd in the Mekhilta (here): \"'If he rises and walks about' — I might think within the house; Scripture says 'outside.' If 'outside' — I might think even one who is bedridden [מתנונה]; Scripture says 'if he rises.'\" And this too is very correct — that Scripture says: if he rises from his bed completely and he walks about continually in the streets, so that he does not return to his bed when he comes in from outside, as is the practice of the bedridden [מתנונים], even though he is weak and leans upon a staff, the striker is acquitted. And the principle is that all this is as it were a parable of what ordinarily happens, and the law is that they assess him for life (Sanhedrin 78b), and therefore Onkelos translates (Onkelos on Exodus 21:19) \"upon his strength [על בוריה].\"" "And Scripture said <b>only his rest shall he give, and he shall surely be healed</b>, and did not say 'his rest and his healing shall he give,' to indicate that he shall give the payment to the physicians and they shall heal him, and he [the injured party] cannot demand that he give him the payment and he will do other things with it — rather, he must be healed with it by all means."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The Aramaic/Aramaic-derived terms מתנונה / מתנונים refer to a bedridden or chronically ailing person; rendered here literally as 'bedridden' in keeping with the cryptic technical usage drawn from the Mekhilta."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:13:37.427274381Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}